H('257.d.5.K.iii',4,"Interest on debt is tax-deductible, so after-tax cost of debt is:","Lower than the stated interest rate",["Higher than the rate","Equal to the tax rate","Zero always"],"The tax shield reduces the cost.")
H('257.d.5.K.iv',3,"When a corporation issues stock, it receives cash and the investor receives:","An ownership share",["A loan note","An insurance policy","A tax credit"],"Stock is equity.")
H('257.d.5.K.iv',4,"An initial public offering is the first time a company:","Sells stock to the general public",["Pays taxes","Hires employees","Opens a bank account"],"IPO means going public.")
H('257.d.5.K.v',3,"Compared with common stock, preferred stock usually has:","Priority for dividends but no voting rights",["Voting rights","Lower claim on assets","No dividend"],"Preferred has dividend priority.")
H('257.d.5.K.v',4,"Common stockholders differ from preferred holders in that common holders:","Receive residual earnings after other claims",["Are paid first always","Have fixed dividends","Cannot vote"],"Common is the residual claim.")
H('257.d.5.K.vi',3,"A preferred stock pays a $6 dividend and sells for $80. Its cost is:","7.5%",["13.3%","6%","80%"],"6/80 = 0.075.")
H('257.d.5.K.vi',4,"A common stock expects a $2 dividend next year, trades at $40, and dividends grow 4% yearly. Its cost is:","9%",["5%","4%","8%"],"2/40 + 4% = 9%.")
H('257.d.5.K.vii',3,"A firm with debt of $400,000 and equity of $200,000 has a debt-to-equity ratio of:","2.0",["0.5","1.0","3.0"],"400,000 / 200,000 = 2.")
H('257.d.5.K.vii',4,"Using more debt (financial leverage) can magnify returns to owners but also increases:","Risk of financial distress",["Safety","Equity","Liquidity ratio"],"Leverage raises risk.")
H('257.d.5.L',3,"A distribution of cash to shareholders from earnings is a:","Cash dividend",["Stock split","Bond coupon","Wage"],"Dividends reward owners.")
H('257.d.5.L',4,"A 10% stock dividend on 1,000 shares gives holders of 1,000 shares:","100 additional shares",["10 additional shares","1,000 additional shares","No shares"],"10% of 1,000 = 100.")
H('257.d.5.M',3,"A firm that pays out a large share of earnings as dividends will more likely need:","External financing for growth",["No funding","Fewer investors","A lower stock price"],"Less retained cash means outside funding.")
H('257.d.5.M',4,"A growing company that retains more earnings generally has:","Smaller external financing needs",["Larger external needs","No profits","No shareholders"],"Retention funds growth internally.")
H('257.d.5.N',3,"Under the residual theory of dividends, a firm first funds:","All acceptable investment projects",["Dividends","Stock repurchases","Officer bonuses"],"Dividends come from what is left.")
H('257.d.5.N',4,"A firm earns $150,000 and needs $120,000 of equity for projects. Under the residual theory, dividends are:","$30,000",["$150,000","$120,000","$270,000"],"150,000 - 120,000 = 30,000.")
H('257.d.5.O',3,"According to the dividend irrelevance theory, firm value depends mainly on:","Investment earnings power rather than payout ratio",["Dividend size","Stock color","Share certificates"],"Modigliani-Miller argues payout does not matter.")
H('257.d.5.O',4,"Investors who prefer current income may favor firms that:","Pay regular dividends",["Never pay dividends","Cut dividends often","Issue no stock"],"Clientele effect.")
H('257.d.5.P',3,"A dividend policy is a firm's plan for:","How much and when to pay shareholders",["Hiring workers","Naming products","Choosing offices"],"Policy guides payouts.")
H('257.d.5.P',4,"A stable dividend policy usually:","Pays a steady amount that signals confidence",["Changes every quarter","Eliminates dividends","Ignores earnings"],"Stability signals strength.")
H('257.d.5.Q',3,"A firm that wants to return cash to owners while giving them flexibility on taxes might choose a:","Stock repurchase",["Bond default","Name change","New logo"],"Buybacks defer taxation.")
H('257.d.5.Q',4,"A stock dividend differs from a cash dividend because it:","Gives more shares instead of cash",["Pays cash","Lowers shares","Cancels the stock"],"Stock dividends issue shares.")
H('257.d.5.R.i',3,"In a merger, two companies:","Combine into one",["Compete forever","Cancel contracts","Split apart"],"Mergers unite firms.")
H('257.d.5.R.i',4,"An acquisition differs from a merger because in an acquisition one company:","Buys another and often keeps it as a subsidiary",["Disappears with no payment","Becomes a charity","Is created from scratch"],"The buyer takes control.")
H('257.d.5.R.ii',3,"A hostile takeover occurs when a bidder:","Pursues control without the target management's approval",["Is invited by the target","Buys inventory","Borrows from a bank"],"Management opposes the bid.")
H('257.d.5.R.ii',4,"A poison pill is a defense that makes a hostile takeover:","More expensive for the bidder",["Cheaper","Faster","Tax-free"],"Pills dilute the bidder's stake.")
H('257.d.5.R.iii',3,"Employees often worry after a merger about:","Job cuts and culture clashes",["Winning prizes","New logos only","Lower rent"],"Integration affects people.")
H('257.d.5.R.iii',4,"One common merger problem is overpaying, which can result in:","Losses that destroy shareholder value",["Guaranteed profits","Lower taxes","Higher dividends always"],"Overpayment hurts returns.")
H('257.d.5.R.iv',3,"One way to value a target is to compare its price-to-earnings ratio to:","Similar companies",["Its landlord","Its mascot","Its vendors' pets"],"Comparables set a benchmark.")
H('257.d.5.R.iv',4,"Estimating the present value of a target's expected future cash flows is called:","Discounted cash flow valuation",["Petty cash","Trial balancing","Closing"],"DCF values cash flows.")
H('257.d.5.R.v',3,"A target is attractive when it has strong cash flow and the purchase price is:","Below its estimated value",["Above its estimated value","Unknown","Zero"],"Buy below value.")
H('257.d.5.R.v',4,"A buyer values a target at $50 million and learns hidden liabilities of $8 million. The adjusted value is:","$42 million",["$58 million","$50 million","$8 million"],"50 - 8 = 42.")
H('257.d.5.R.vi',3,"Selling a division to focus on core businesses is a:","Divestiture",["Merger","IPO","Dividend"],"Divestitures shrink the firm.")
H('257.d.5.R.vi',4,"Creating a separate public company from a division and giving its shares to current owners is a:","Spin-off",["Stock split","Bond issue","Audit"],"Spin-offs separate units.")
H('257.d.6.A.i',3,"After accounting scandals, the Sarbanes-Oxley Act strengthened:","Auditor independence and executive accountability",["Retail pricing","School funding","Zoning rules"],"SOX targeted fraud.")
H('257.d.6.A.i',4,"The Public Company Accounting Oversight Board, created by Sarbanes-Oxley, oversees:","Auditors of public companies",["Restaurants","Car dealers","Banks' tellers"],"PCAOB regulates audit firms.")
H('257.d.6.A.ii',3,"The SEC regulates accounting for public companies by requiring:","Audited filings that follow set standards",["Secret books","No audits","Handwritten ledgers"],"The SEC enforces disclosure.")
H('257.d.6.A.ii',4,"The SEC has authority over accounting standards but has historically delegated their setting to the:","Financial Accounting Standards Board",["Texas legislature","IRS","Federal Reserve"],"FASB sets GAAP.")
H('257.d.6.A.iii',3,"State regulation of accounting includes:","Licensing CPAs and disciplining misconduct",["Setting stock prices","Printing tax forms","Banning audits"],"States license accountants.")
H('257.d.6.A.iii',4,"To become a CPA in Texas, a candidate must meet education, pass the exam, and satisfy:","Experience and ethics requirements",["Residency in a dorm","A cooking test","Retirement age"],"Licensing has several parts.")
H('257.d.6.A.iv',3,"A key difference between IFRS and U.S. GAAP is that IFRS is generally more:","Principles-based",["Rules-based","Secret","Optional"],"IFRS gives broad principles.")
H('257.d.6.A.iv',4,"Under IFRS, the inventory method that is not allowed is:","LIFO",["FIFO","Weighted average","Specific identification"],"IFRS prohibits LIFO.")
H('257.d.6.B.i',3,"An accountant investigating possible fraud starts by writing:","Questions about what happened and how it was hidden",["Press releases","Tax returns","Sales slogans"],"Questions guide inquiry.")
H('257.d.6.B.i',4,"Which question best guides a case study of a revenue recognition scandal?","Which transactions were recorded early and why?",["What color is the logo?","Who is the oldest worker?","How big is the lobby?"],"Focus on the accounting issue.")
H('257.d.6.B.ii',3,"Relevant sources for a fraud case study include:","Court filings and SEC enforcement releases",["Fan posts","Memes","Rumors"],"Official records are key.")
H('257.d.6.B.ii',4,"Evidence gathered in a fraud case should be:","Documented and traceable to its source",["Memorized only","Unlabeled","Verbal only"],"Documentation supports credibility.")
H('257.d.6.B.iii',3,"A source is more valid and reliable when it is:","Written by an expert and supported by evidence",["Anonymous","Unsourced","Written to sell something"],"Expertise and evidence matter.")
H('257.d.6.B.iii',4,"A researcher finds two accounts of a company's restatement that conflict. The best step is to:","Compare them with the company's SEC filing",["Choose the shorter one","Ignore both","Trust the funnier one"],"Primary filings resolve conflicts.")
H('257.d.6.B.iv',3,"After describing a fraud, the analyst should identify which:","Laws and regulations were violated or apply",["Songs are popular","Colors were used","Foods were served"],"Link facts to law.")
H('257.d.6.B.iv',4,"Falsely reporting revenue to investors could violate:","Securities fraud provisions",["Parking ordinances","Zoning laws","Jaywalking rules"],"Misstatements break securities laws.")
H('257.d.6.B.v',3,"Evidence of fraud in the books may include:","Unusual journal entries made near period end",["Neat handwriting","Regular schedules","Blank pages"],"Odd entries are red flags.")
H('257.d.6.B.v',4,"A pattern of receivables rising much faster than sales may indicate:","Possible revenue overstatement",["Strong collections","Lower taxes","Greater cash"],"It can signal fictitious sales.")
H('257.d.6.B.vi',3,"A clear case study presentation should include correct grammar, spelling, and:","Citations of the sources used",["Unrelated art","Random facts","No conclusion"],"Citations credit sources.")
H('257.d.6.B.vi',4,"Citing sources in a presentation helps the audience:","Check the evidence themselves",["Avoid reading","Remove facts","Skip slides"],"Transparency builds trust.")
H('257.d.7.A',3,"An auditor uses data mining software to find:","Duplicate payments and unusual patterns",["New customers","New logos","Weather data"],"Software searches big data sets.")
H('257.d.7.A',4,"Running an analysis on 100% of transactions instead of a sample improves the chance of:","Detecting anomalies",["Missing errors","Raising errors","Losing records"],"Full-population testing finds more issues.")
H('257.d.7.B',3,"A presentation of accounting results to nonaccountants should use:","Clear language and supporting visuals",["Only jargon","No data","Unlabeled charts"],"Clarity helps the audience.")
H('257.d.7.B',4,"A written report on a ratio analysis should state the findings and:","Recommendations based on them",["Personal stories","Unrelated facts","Jokes"],"Reports end with action.")

# ---- 258 Financial Analysis
H('258.d.2',3,"A budget is a tool used to:","Plan and control how money is used",["Print currency","Set interest rates","Elect officers"],"Budgets plan resources.")
H('258.d.2',4,"Comparing actual spending to the budget each month is a way to:","Monitor and control financial resources",["Cancel projects","Avoid records","Raise taxes"],"Monitoring enables control.")
H('258.d.3',3,"A table of monthly sales for a year can be turned into a line graph to show:","Trends over time",["Employee names","Tax law","Logos"],"Graphs show patterns.")
H('258.d.3',4,"Monthly expenses of $4,000, $4,200, $3,800, and $4,000 have an average of:","$4,000",["$4,200","$16,000","$3,800"],"16,000 / 4 = 4,000.")
H('258.d.1.A',3,"The scope of accounting includes recording, classifying, summarizing, and:","Interpreting financial information",["Selling products","Hiring","Advertising"],"Accounting covers the whole process.")
H('258.d.1.A',4,"Which branch of accounting prepares reports for outside users such as investors?","Financial accounting",["Managerial accounting","Cost accounting only","Personal budgeting"],"Financial accounting serves outsiders.")
H('258.d.1.B',3,"Tracking money flow means recording where cash comes from and:","Where it goes",["Which songs play","Who works late","What time it is"],"Cash flow is inflow and outflow.")
H('258.d.1.B',4,"A business collects $90,000 and pays $75,000 in a month. Net cash flow is:","+$15,000",["-$15,000","+$165,000","+$90,000"],"90,000 - 75,000 = 15,000.")
H('258.d.4.A',3,"A budget is mainly a:","Plan of expected revenue and expenses",["Record of past errors","Tax form","Loan note"],"Budgets plan ahead.")
H('258.d.4.A',4,"A zero-based budget requires managers to:","Justify every expense from scratch each period",["Copy last year","Spend nothing","Ignore goals"],"Zero-based starts at zero.")
H('258.d.4.B',3,"Marginal revenue is the:","Added revenue from selling one more unit",["Total of all revenue","Cost of one unit","Average price"],"Marginal means additional.")
H('258.d.4.B',4,"A firm maximizes profit where marginal revenue equals:","Marginal cost",["Total cost","Fixed cost","Price"],"MR = MC is the rule.")
H('258.d.4.C',3,"Net income of $20,000 on sales of $200,000 gives a net profit margin of:","10%",["1%","20%","100%"],"20,000/200,000 = 0.10.")
H('258.d.4.C',4,"Current assets of $60,000 and current liabilities of $30,000 give a current ratio of:","2.0",["0.5","3.0","1.5"],"60,000/30,000 = 2.")
H('258.d.4.D',3,"To judge a company's solvency, a reader would look mostly at the:","Balance sheet",["Mission statement","Logo","Office layout"],"The balance sheet lists assets and debts.")
H('258.d.4.D',4,"A rising net income but falling operating cash flow may indicate:","Earnings quality problems",["Strong health","No issue","Higher liquidity"],"Profit without cash is a warning.")
H('258.d.4.E',3,"Comparing each line of an income statement to a percentage of sales is called:","Vertical (common-size) analysis",["Horizontal analysis","Break-even analysis","Trial balance"],"Common-size compares to a base.")
H('258.d.4.E',4,"Comparing a company's results across several years to see changes is:","Trend (horizontal) analysis",["Vertical analysis","Ratio rounding","Bank reconciliation"],"Horizontal analysis tracks change.")
H('258.d.4.F',3,"Sudden large increases in receivables with flat sales are a possible:","Red flag about the financial statements",["Sign of strong sales","Tax credit","Dividend"],"Unusual patterns need questions.")
H('258.d.4.F',4,"Off-balance-sheet obligations can mislead readers because they:","Hide debts from the main statements",["Add assets","Increase cash","Lower taxes"],"Hidden debt hides risk.")
H('258.d.5.A',3,"Business finance uses ratios to:","Compare and judge performance and risk",["Create invoices","Print checks","Hire managers"],"Ratios summarize health.")
H('258.d.5.A',4,"Ratios are most useful when compared with industry averages and:","The company's past results",["Next week's weather","Employee ages","Office size"],"Trends and benchmarks give meaning.")
H('258.d.5.B',3,"The Altman Z-score is a ratio-based tool used for:","Bankruptcy prediction",["Hiring","Marketing","Inventory counting"],"Z-score predicts distress.")
H('258.d.5.B',4,"Times interest earned of 1.1 suggests a company:","Barely covers its interest payments",["Has abundant cover","Has no debt","Is risk-free"],"Low coverage is risky.")
H('258.d.5.C',3,"A benchmark ratio is useful because it provides:","A standard for comparison",["A guaranteed profit","A tax refund","A legal contract"],"Benchmarks set a reference.")
H('258.d.5.C',4,"A company's inventory turnover is 4 while its industry's is 8. The comparison suggests:","Slower-moving inventory than peers",["Faster sales than peers","No difference","Higher profit for certain"],"Lower turnover means slower sales.")
H('258.d.6.A',3,"Analyzing a company's financial situation using its statements involves reading the balance sheet, income statement, and:","Cash flow statement",["Menu","Calendar","Lease only"],"The three statements together tell the story.")
H('258.d.6.A',4,"A company with rising sales, shrinking margins, and growing debt likely faces:","Pressure on profitability and solvency",["A comfortable future","No risk","Lower costs"],"Combined trends reveal strain.")
H('258.d.6.B',3,"An external force that can change a company's value is:","A rise in interest rates",["A new paint color","A staff birthday","A broken printer"],"Rates affect financing costs.")
H('258.d.6.B',4,"New environmental regulations on a manufacturer are an example of an external:","Legal and regulatory force",["Internal choice","Marketing step","Accounting method"],"Regulation changes cost structure.")
H('258.d.6.C',3,"A company creates value when its return on invested capital exceeds its:","Cost of capital",["Tax rate","Payroll","Rent"],"Value comes from returns above the cost of funds.")
H('258.d.6.C',4,"Economic value added is positive when after-tax operating profit is greater than:","The charge for the capital used",["Sales","Zero cost","Assets"],"EVA subtracts a capital charge.")
H('258.d.6.D',3,"The notes to financial statements explain items such as:","Accounting policies and contingent liabilities",["Employee names","Parking","Slogans"],"Notes give required details.")
H('258.d.6.D',4,"A note disclosing a pending lawsuit helps readers judge:","Possible future losses",["Past revenue","Employee pay","Color schemes"],"Contingencies affect risk.")
H('258.d.6.E',3,"A creditor evaluating results is most interested in whether the company:","Can repay its debts",["Has a nice logo","Is growing in followers","Pays executives well"],"Creditors focus on repayment.")
H('258.d.6.E',4,"Investors evaluating the same results are most interested in:","Earnings growth and return on investment",["Collateral only","Credit limit only","Office rent"],"Investors seek returns.")
H('258.d.7.A',3,"A lock on the cash room is an internal control, while a bank's outside audit is an:","External control",["Internal control","Error","Marketing step"],"External checks come from outside.")
H('258.d.7.A',4,"Risk control includes policies, procedures, and:","Monitoring that they are followed",["Ignoring results","Raising risk","Skipping review"],"Controls need monitoring.")
H('258.d.7.B',3,"A common way to assess a risk is to rate its likelihood and its:","Potential impact",["Color","Name","Length"],"Risk = likelihood x impact.")
H('258.d.7.B',4,"A risk with high likelihood and high impact should be:","Addressed first",["Ignored","Delayed","Accepted without review"],"Prioritize severe risks.")
H('258.d.7.C',3,"Auditing risk control means checking whether controls:","Work as designed",["Exist on paper only","Are decorative","Are optional"],"Audits test effectiveness.")
H('258.d.7.C',4,"An audit that finds a control gap should lead to:","A corrective action plan",["No response","Blame only","A new logo"],"Gaps are fixed.")
H('258.d.7.D',3,"A system of checks such as passwords, approvals, and audit logs is a:","Risk-control system",["Marketing plan","Dividend policy","Job posting"],"Layered controls protect assets.")
H('258.d.7.D',4,"Requiring two approvals for large wire transfers is an example of:","Preventive control",["Detective control only","No control","Corrective control only"],"Prevention stops problems before they occur.")
H('258.d.7.E',3,"Technology helps risk management by:","Monitoring transactions for unusual activity",["Hiding data","Removing controls","Raising costs only"],"Software flags anomalies.")
H('258.d.7.E',4,"Encrypting customer data reduces the risk of:","Data theft and breach costs",["Higher sales","Lower rent","More audits"],"Encryption protects data.")
H('258.d.7.F',3,"Laws such as data privacy rules affect risk management because violating them can lead to:","Fines and lawsuits",["Bonuses","Lower costs","Tax credits"],"Noncompliance has penalties.")
H('258.d.7.F',4,"Companies manage legal risk by:","Training staff and monitoring compliance",["Ignoring laws","Hiding errors","Delaying filings"],"Compliance programs reduce exposure.")
H('258.d.7.G',3,"Taking on a risky project can be reasonable if it supports:","The company's objectives and expected return",["Nothing","A coin flip","A rumor"],"Risk should serve goals.")
H('258.d.7.G',4,"A company's risk appetite describes the:","Amount of risk it is willing to accept to reach objectives",["Number of employees","Size of the logo","Rent paid"],"Appetite guides decisions.")
H('258.d.7.H',3,"To evaluate a risk-management program, managers review whether losses have:","Decreased relative to expectations",["Increased steadily","Been ignored","Been hidden"],"Results show effectiveness.")
H('258.d.7.H',4,"A good evaluation of a risk program compares costs of controls to:","The losses they prevent",["The logo","Last year's slogan","The office size"],"Benefits must justify costs.")
H('258.d.8.A',3,"The main objective of an annual report is to:","Inform stakeholders about performance and plans",["Entertain readers","Promote vacations","Replace tax forms"],"Reports communicate results.")
H('258.d.8.A',4,"Management uses the letter to shareholders to:","Explain results and outlook in its own words",["Hide results","Describe menus","List employee salaries"],"The letter gives narrative context.")
H('258.d.8.B',3,"A typical annual report includes financial statements, notes, and the:","Independent auditor's report",["Parking map","Recipe","Advertising jingle"],"The audit opinion is a core part.")
H('258.d.8.B',4,"The section that discusses results, risks, and liquidity in management's words is the:","Management's discussion and analysis",["Footnote to a lease","Cover page","Table of contents"],"MD&A is required narrative.")
H('258.d.8.C',3,"Publicly traded companies must file annual reports with the:","Securities and Exchange Commission",["Post office","School board","Motor vehicle department"],"The SEC receives the 10-K.")
H('258.d.8.C',4,"Executives must certify the accuracy of annual financial reports under the:","Sarbanes-Oxley Act",["Homestead Act","Clayton Act","Social Security Act"],"SOX adds certification.")
H('258.d.8.D',3,"A student building a sample annual report should begin by gathering the company's:","Financial statements and key facts",["Gossip","Pictures of pets","Song lists"],"Start with data.")
H('258.d.8.D',4,"Before finalizing an annual report, the preparer should check that totals on statements:","Agree with each other and the notes",["Look attractive","Are rounded randomly","Are hidden"],"Consistency prevents errors.")
H('258.d.9.A',3,"Business ethics refers to:","Principles of right and wrong in business conduct",["Tax tables","Marketing slogans","Legal fees"],"Ethics guides behavior.")
H('258.d.9.A',4,"An ethical business that discovers a product flaw should:","Tell customers and fix it",["Hide it","Blame buyers","Raise prices"],"Honesty protects stakeholders.")
H('258.d.9.B',3,"Ethics matters in finance because investors and the public rely on:","Honest, accurate financial information",["Flashy ads","Rumors","Luck"],"Trust is the basis of markets.")
H('258.d.9.B',4,"A finance manager pressured to inflate earnings to hit a bonus target should:","Refuse and report the pressure",["Comply quietly","Delete records","Blame the auditor"],"Integrity over incentives.")
H('258.d.9.C',3,"Enron used special purpose entities to:","Hide debt and inflate its reported financial health",["Reduce taxes legally","Increase transparency","Lower payroll"],"Off-books entities masked debt.")
H('258.d.9.C',4,"Enron's auditor, which was also implicated and ceased operating after the scandal, was:","Arthur Andersen",["Ernst & Young","Moody's","Goldman Sachs"],"Arthur Andersen collapsed in 2002.")
H('258.d.10.A',3,"Which common task requires an employee to solve a problem?","Locating the reason a ledger does not balance",["Opening the mail","Walking to a meeting","Watering a plant"],"Troubleshooting uses problem solving.")
H('258.d.10.A',4,"An employee who resolves a customer's billing dispute is using skills such as:","Analysis and communication",["Guessing","Delay","Avoidance"],"Problem solving combines skills.")
H('258.d.10.B',3,"Breaking a financial problem into smaller parts helps develop:","Creative and workable solutions",["Confusion","Errors","Delays"],"Decomposition clarifies.")
H('258.d.10.B',4,"A firm with cash shortages in summer might creatively solve it by:","Negotiating seasonal credit terms with suppliers",["Ignoring bills","Hiding invoices","Raising rent"],"Terms can smooth cash flow.")
H('258.d.10.C',3,"After proposing a solution to a financial problem, you should evaluate:","Whether it solves the problem within constraints",["The font","The colors","The slogan"],"Test proposals against goals.")
H('258.d.10.C',4,"A proposal to cut costs by 20% should be evaluated for its effect on:","Quality and long-term sales",["Only this month's lunch","Only the logo","Office colors"],"Cuts have side effects.")
H('258.d.10.D',3,"Financial information is valuable to a problem solver when it is:","Relevant, accurate, and timely",["Old and vague","Unverified","Unrelated"],"Good data supports decisions.")
H('258.d.10.D',4,"Outdated sales data used to plan next quarter's inventory could lead to:","Overstock or stockouts",["Perfect planning","Lower costs always","Higher profits for sure"],"Stale data misleads.")
H('258.d.10.E',3,"Using tools such as spreadsheets, decision trees, and cost-benefit analysis helps compare:","Alternative financial solutions",["Colors","Logos","Fonts"],"Tools structure comparison.")
H('258.d.10.E',4,"A cost-benefit analysis recommends the option with:","Benefits that exceed costs by the greatest margin",["The lowest cost only","The highest cost only","The longest name"],"Net benefit decides.")
H('258.d.11.A',3,"A company analysis begins by summarizing the company's:","Background, products, and markets",["Employees' hobbies","Executives' pets","Favorite foods"],"Background sets context.")
H('258.d.11.A',4,"Which source is best for a company's official background?","Its annual report or SEC filings",["An anonymous blog","A rumor site","A joke page"],"Primary filings are reliable.")
H('258.d.11.B',3,"Analyzing a company's history shows how it:","Developed and grew over time",["Chose colors","Hired pets","Named streets"],"History reveals growth drivers.")
H('258.d.11.B',4,"A company that grew mainly by acquisition would show history of:","Repeated purchases of other firms",["No changes","Only layoffs","Only price cuts"],"Strategy can be read from history.")
H('258.d.11.C',3,"An internal strength of a firm might be:","A strong brand and skilled workforce",["A new competitor","A recession","A new regulation"],"Internal factors are within the firm.")
H('258.d.11.C',4,"Heavy debt and outdated equipment are examples of internal:","Weaknesses",["Opportunities","Threats","Strengths"],"They limit performance.")
H('258.d.11.D',3,"The external environment includes competitors, customers, and:","Economic and regulatory conditions",["Employee lunches","Office decor","Break rooms"],"External forces affect the firm.")
H('258.d.11.D',4,"A new competitor entering the market is part of the company's:","External environment",["Internal strengths","Budget","Payroll"],"Competition is external.")
H('258.d.11.E',3,"In a SWOT analysis, 'O' stands for:","Opportunities",["Operations","Outputs","Overhead"],"SWOT = strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats.")
H('258.d.11.E',4,"A growing market for a company's product is a:","Opportunity",["Weakness","Threat","Strength"],"Market growth is external and positive.")
H('258.d.11.F',3,"Corporate-level strategy decides which:","Businesses the company should be in",["Brand colors","Break times","Phone numbers"],"Corporate strategy sets scope.")
